From bd605eaf288d6732625f71c9943fff1d5a21f0fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:47:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] trans: size_of_val of a nested DST adds the offsets of every
 wrapper level, rounding each one

librsvg 2.58.5 (the Rust librsvg port), rsvg-convert at run time, while parsing the first CSS rule:

    thread 'main' panicked at :0:0:
    assertion failed: ((((buffer).add(size,) as usize) - ((current as *mut u8) as usize)) < inner_align)

servo_arc 0.3.0 lib.rs:760, `Arc::<HeaderSlice<HeaderWithLength<SpecificityAndFlags>,
[Component<Impl>]>>::from_header_and_iter_alloc` (selectors 0.25.0 parse_selector, from rsvg's
css.rs Stylesheet::add_rules_from_string).  servo_arc sizes the allocation with

    mem::size_of_val(&*(slice::from_raw_parts(..) as *const [T] as *const ArcInner<HeaderSlice<H, [T]>>))

where `ArcInner<T: ?Sized> { count: AtomicUsize, data: T }` and `HeaderSlice<H, T: ?Sized>
{ header: H, slice: T }`: the unsized tail is two structs deep.  The size_of_val intrinsic took
the last-field offset of the outermost struct only (`ALIGN_TO(4 + n * size, align)` on ppc32), so
the header (HeaderWithLength<SpecificityAndFlags>, 12 bytes) was missing: the allocation was 12
bytes short (a heap overflow when the items are written) and servo_arc's debug check of the slack
`buffer + size - end of the last item` wrapped around.

size_of_val now walks the chain of last fields down to the slice/str/trait object and computes
rustc's size_and_align_of_dst: at each level `offset of the tail + size of the inner level`, rounded
up to that level's alignment, which is size_of of the same type with a `[T; N]` tail (also under the
Darwin ppc32 field-alignment cap, where an inner level can be more aligned than the outer one).  The
rounding is only emitted where it can change the value, so single-level types (Arc<str>, Rc<[T]>,
Path/OsStr/CStr) produce the same C as before.  A trait object tail gets the offsets of all levels
summed (it had the outermost one only); its alignment rules are unchanged here.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context)
